# Cordon image: the startup-order contract.
#
# mkqnximage generates the full IFS build file for the image (output/build/
# ifs.build in the build directory) from the SDP's own templates. What Cordon
# adds to that image, and the order it adds it in, is this file. It is the thing
# scenario C5 is about: an image that starts a client before the service exists
# must be caught, and the only difference between the two images built by
# target/boot/build-g2-image.sh is these lines.
#
# Normal image, appended to post_startup.sh (the last thing startup.sh runs):
#
#   1. secpolpush                      push /proc/boot/secpol.bin to procnto
#   2. on -T cordon_t cordon-supervise start the supervisor, which starts
#                                      /proc/boot/cordon as its child; the child
#                                      attaches /dev/cordon
#   3. wait for /dev/cordon            the run does not continue without it
#   4. on -T cordon_view_t cordon-client status
#                                      first client of the run; it must be
#                                      answered (startup-order check)
#   5. deny / allow / kill-consumer / supervised-restart scenarios, in that order
#   6. slog2info -S, then CORDON-G2-COMPLETE
#
# Misordered image, same file with steps 2 and 4 swapped:
#
#   1. secpolpush
#   2. on -T cordon_view_t cordon-client status
#                                      first client runs before the service
#                                      exists: it must fail
#   3. on -T cordon_t cordon-supervise, wait for /dev/cordon, then the same
#      client again: it must succeed, which shows the image is otherwise fine
#
# Every process the scenarios depend on is started with an explicit security
# type by `on -T`, because the policy is pushed before any of them starts.
#
# The pieces themselves (cordon, cordon-supervise, cordon-client, cordon-view,
# cordon-boot.sh, secpol.bin) are put into the IFS by the mkqnximage snippets
# that target/boot/build-g2-image.sh writes.
